      modelAnswer: 'In a physical change, such as ice melting or salt dissolving in water, no new substances are formed. The particles themselves stay the same; only their arrangement, spacing and movement change as forces between particles are overcome or formed. Physical changes are usually easy to reverse — water can be frozen back into ice and salt can be recovered by evaporating the water. In a chemical change, such as magnesium burning in oxygen, bonds between atoms are broken and new bonds are made, so the atoms are rearranged into new substances (here magnesium oxide) with different properties. Chemical changes are usually difficult to reverse and are often accompanied by an energy change, a colour change or a gas being given off. In both types of change, atoms are neither created nor destroyed, so mass is conserved.'
