      modelAnswer: 'The simple particle model is useful because it explains many observations about states of matter. It shows why solids have a fixed shape (particles in fixed positions), why liquids flow (particles can slide past each other) and why gases can be compressed (there are large spaces between particles). It also explains why energy is needed to melt or boil a substance, because the particles must move more freely. However, the model has important limitations. Representing particles as hard spheres like bowling balls ignores the forces of attraction between particles, yet it is the strength of these forces that determines the temperature at which a substance melts or boils; the model therefore cannot explain why different substances have different melting points. The model also does not take into account the actual size of the particles, which are not all the same and are not really solid balls, and it does not show the spaces between particles to scale — in a gas the gaps are far larger than usually drawn. Particles may also be molecules made of several atoms rather than single spheres. Overall, the model is a good starting point for describing changes of state, but it needs to include intermolecular forces and realistic particle sizes and spacing to explain them fully.'
