      modelAnswer: 'In the early 1800s John Dalton proposed that all matter is made of tiny atoms that cannot be divided, and that atoms of the same element are identical; he pictured atoms as solid spheres. In 1897 J. J. Thomson discovered the electron, a very small negatively charged particle that could be removed from atoms. This showed that atoms are not indivisible, so Thomson proposed the plum pudding model, in which the atom is a sphere of positive charge with negative electrons embedded in it. Geiger and Marsden, working with Rutherford, then fired positively charged alpha particles at very thin gold foil. Most alpha particles passed straight through, but some were deflected and a very small number bounced back. The plum pudding model could not explain this, because its spread-out positive charge would not deflect alpha particles strongly. Rutherford concluded that most of the atom is empty space and that the positive charge and almost all of the mass are concentrated in a tiny central nucleus, with electrons around it — the nuclear model. Niels Bohr then refined this by proposing that electrons orbit the nucleus in fixed shells, or energy levels, at specific distances, which agreed with experimental observations of the light emitted by atoms. Later work showed that the nucleus contains protons and neutrons. Each change happened because new experimental evidence could not be explained by the existing model, showing how scientific models are tested and replaced.'
