      modelAnswer: 'Ionic compounds, such as sodium chloride, form when metal atoms transfer electrons to non-metal atoms, producing positive and negative ions with full outer shells. These ions are arranged in a giant lattice held together by strong electrostatic forces of attraction acting in all directions between oppositely charged ions. Simple molecules, such as water, methane and oxygen, are formed when non-metal atoms share pairs of electrons in covalent bonds. The covalent bonds within each molecule are strong, but the molecules contain only a fixed, small number of atoms and are held to each other by weak intermolecular forces. Giant covalent structures, such as diamond, graphite and silicon dioxide, also contain covalent bonds, but every atom is bonded to others in a continuous three-dimensional network, so there are no separate molecules and very many strong bonds. Polymers consist of very long chain molecules with covalent bonds along the chain and intermolecular forces between chains, which are stronger than between small molecules. Metals consist of a giant lattice of positive metal ions surrounded by a sea of delocalised electrons, and are held together by strong electrostatic attraction between the ions and the delocalised electrons. So ionic and metallic bonding both involve electrostatic attraction in giant lattices, whereas covalent bonding involves shared electron pairs and can form either small molecules or giant structures.'
