      modelAnswer: 'Diamond, graphite and graphene are all forms of carbon with giant covalent structures. In diamond every carbon atom forms four strong covalent bonds to other carbon atoms in a rigid three-dimensional tetrahedral lattice. A very large amount of energy is needed to break these bonds, so diamond has a very high melting point and is extremely hard, which makes it useful for cutting tools. Diamond has no delocalised electrons, so it does not conduct electricity. In graphite each carbon atom forms three covalent bonds, giving flat layers of hexagonal rings. The fourth electron from each atom is delocalised and can move along the layers, so graphite conducts electricity, which is why it is used for electrodes. The layers are held together only by weak forces, so they can slide over each other, making graphite soft and slippery and useful as a lubricant; it still has a very high melting point because the covalent bonds within the layers must be broken. Graphene is a single layer of graphite, one atom thick. Its strong covalent bonds make it extremely strong yet very light, and its delocalised electrons make it an excellent conductor, so it has potential uses in electronics and composites.'
