      modelAnswer: 'The melting point of a substance depends on how much energy is needed to overcome the forces holding its particles together. Ionic compounds such as sodium chloride have giant lattices with strong electrostatic forces between oppositely charged ions acting in all directions, so a lot of energy is needed to separate the ions and they have high melting points. They do not conduct when solid because the ions are fixed in place, but they conduct when molten or dissolved because the ions are then free to move and carry charge. Simple molecular substances such as methane have strong covalent bonds within molecules, but only weak intermolecular forces between molecules; melting only requires these weak forces to be overcome, so melting and boiling points are low and increase with molecular size. They do not conduct electricity because the molecules have no overall charge and there are no free electrons or ions. Metals have strong electrostatic attraction between positive ions and delocalised electrons, so most have high melting points, and they conduct electricity in both solid and liquid states because the delocalised electrons can move through the structure. The properties of the bulk material arise from the many particles together, not from individual atoms.'
