      marks: 9, command: 'Explain',
      modelAnswer: 'In an exothermic reaction energy is transferred to the surroundings, so the temperature of the surroundings rises; examples include combustion and neutralisation. In an endothermic reaction energy is taken in from the surroundings, so their temperature falls, as in thermal decomposition. During a reaction, bonds in the reactants must be broken, which requires energy and is endothermic, and new bonds are made in the products, which releases energy and is exothermic. If more energy is released when new bonds form than is needed to break the original bonds, the overall reaction is exothermic; if more energy is needed to break bonds than is released in making them, the reaction is endothermic. A reaction profile plots energy against the progress of the reaction. For an exothermic reaction the products are drawn lower than the reactants, and the difference is the energy released; for an endothermic reaction the products are higher than the reactants. In both cases the curve rises to a peak first, and the height from the reactants to the peak is the activation energy, the minimum energy the particles need to collide successfully and react.'
