      modelAnswer: 'Oxidation can be described as the gain of oxygen and reduction as the loss of oxygen. In the reaction 2CuO + C → 2Cu + CO₂, carbon gains oxygen and is oxidised, while copper oxide loses oxygen and is reduced. Carbon is the reducing agent because it removes oxygen from the copper oxide, and copper oxide is the oxidising agent. Because oxidation and reduction always happen together, these are called redox reactions. A more general definition uses electrons: oxidation is the loss of electrons and reduction is the gain of electrons. When zinc is added to copper sulfate solution, zinc atoms lose two electrons to form zinc ions, Zn → Zn²⁺ + 2e⁻, so zinc is oxidised, while copper ions gain those electrons to form copper atoms, Cu²⁺ + 2e⁻ → Cu, so copper ions are reduced. The ionic equation is Zn + Cu²⁺ → Zn²⁺ + Cu. Similarly, when magnesium reacts with chlorine, magnesium is oxidised as it loses electrons to form Mg²⁺ and chlorine is reduced as it gains electrons to form Cl⁻ ions, even though no oxygen is involved, which shows why the electron definition is more widely useful.'
